european central bank vice president  victor constancio had some good news for  investors when he presented the findings  the simulation looked at a snapshot of  banking assets as of the end of 2013  at that time 25 banks were dangerously  low on capital reserves since then 12 of  those banks have recapitalized to deal  with the problem  but there are then 13 banks that still  have either to exactly apply  their restructuring downsize or they  will have to come up with ways to  increase their capital  the ecb test also simulated a global  financial crisis the result 13 european  banks would be at risk of folding in the  event of a severe meltdown those  institutions are located in eight  countries across europe they'll now have  two weeks to tell european banking  authorities how they plan to increase  their capital buffers  several countries including spain  received unexpectedly good marks spain's  economy was especially hard hit during  the housing crisis in 2008.  banks which failed the ecb's stress test  now have nine months to increase their  capital buffers
 

